@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+---
+decision_id: cd-1-lateness-dedupe
+status: accepted
+question: How is an event judged late, and how are replayed events kept from counting twice
+choice: Late when the watermark before the event is more than 10 minutes past its window end, compared strictly. Dedupe on meta.id, first arrival wins, for every recentchange type. A duplicate does not move the watermark. The watermark is the max event time over first arrivals of all recentchange types that carry a timestamp, late ones included, and it is stored in the commit. Non-edit events move the watermark and are deduped, but they are not stored: each commit writes ids.parquet with the meta.id of every first arrival, and the non-edit counts sit in _consumer.json. Q7 counts late edits (rows of late_events.parquet). Late non-edit events are counted in _consumer.json as late_other. event_id = meta.id.
+rationale: The rule measures lateness in stream time, so the replay speed does not matter. The watermark is stored so that a restart gives the same late count. The option rejected is a wall-clock test, which the rule forbids.
