+# Decisions
+
+Each decision record lives in `.context/proposals/cd-1-<slug>.md`. This file holds the
+evidence for each record. Phase 10 adds the full write-up of D1 to D11 and the assumptions.
+
+## Source profile
+
+Profiling ran in a scratch directory outside the repo. Nothing was extracted into `data/`.
+Counts come from reading the zips with `zipfile` and the CSVs with `csv`.
+
+### USAspending (8 zips: 4 agencies, contracts and assistance)
+
+| Measure | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Rows (total / contracts / assistance) | 9,820 / 416 / 9,404 |
+| `action_date` range | 2023-10-01 to 2024-09-30. 0 rows outside FY2024 |
+| Obligation field | `federal_action_obligation`. Plain decimal `-?\d+\.\d\d`. 0 values with more than 2 decimal places. 0 blank or non-numeric |
+| Negative obligation rows | 1,226 (contracts 37, assistance 1,189) |
+| Duplicate transaction keys | 0. Keys are `contract_transaction_unique_key` and `assistance_transaction_unique_key` |
+| Award key (check 3) | `contract_award_unique_key` and `assistance_award_unique_key`. 7,918 awards. 1,122 awards have a negative net obligation |
+| Place-of-performance county blank | 7,781 rows (79.2%). These map to the `UNKNOWN` member (D4) |
+| Place-of-performance state blank | 57 rows. These are also county-blank rows, so they map to `UNKNOWN` (D4) |
+| County code shape | 5-digit FIPS (state 2 digits + county 3 digits). The state field agrees with the county prefix for every row that has a county |
+
+Join of the county FIPS to the Census files:
+
+| Match | Rows | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| In `co-est2024` (county row) | 2,000 | Population from the 2024 file |
+| Only in `co-est2021` | 16 | All Connecticut, old county codes 09001, 09003, 09005, 09009, 09011, 09013. No population (see below) |
+| In neither file | 23 | States 60 (AS), 66 (GU), 69 (MP), 72 (PR), 78 (VI). No census row. No population |
+
+Month for check 4 (Phase 9, Q4): **2024-07**. It has 609 county rows and 1,021 rows with no county, so
+both county-level and state-level sums are exercised. The next largest is 2024-06 (1,240 no-county rows, 117 county rows).
+
+### Census population (`co-est2024-alldata.csv`, `co-est2021-alldata.csv`)
+
+| Measure | 2024 file | 2021 file |
+|---|---|---|
+| Rows | 3,195 | 3,194 |
+| State summary rows (`SUMLEV` 040, `COUNTY` 000) | 51 | 51 |
+| County-level rows (`SUMLEV` 050, `COUNTY` != 000) | 3,144 | 3,143 |
+| County rows with blank `POPESTIMATE2024` | 0 | n/a |
+| Keys (`STATE` + `COUNTY`) distinct | 3,144 of 3,144 | 3,143 |
+
+- Connecticut: the 2024 file lists nine planning regions (`COUNTY` 110, 120, 130, 140, 150, 160,
+  170, 180, 190) and no counties. The 2021 file lists eight counties (001 to 015, odd codes).
+  The 2021 file has no other state with codes absent from 2024. Count of 2021-only codes per state: Connecticut 8, all other states 0.
+- Population rule (plan MF-10, which overrides the D5 row of the design table): `stg_census_county_population` reads the 2024
+  file only. A county with no 2024 value takes no 2021 value. Connecticut counties (all eight of the old codes) therefore have a
+  **null population**. Q2 does not rank them. The 16 USAspending rows coded to Connecticut's old counties carry no population.
+  Count of 2024-file counties with a blank 2024 value: 0. The same-file fallback to 2023 is therefore never used on this data, but the rule stays in the model.
+- Connecticut planning regions: the staging rule drops the nine 2024 planning-region rows (`STATE` 09, `COUNTY` 110 to 190), as the proposal requires. Eight USAspending rows are coded to three of them (09110, 09170, 09180), so those rows also have a null population. The Phase 6 model `dim_place` shows these as null-population counties.
+- Join key: `STATE` (2 digits, zero-padded) and `COUNTY` (3 digits, zero-padded). Joined to USAspending as the 5-digit FIPS.
+- Encoding: both Census CSVs contain non-UTF-8 bytes (for example 0xF1 in a county name). They are read as Latin-1. Phase 3 config
+  must accept an `encoding` setting for file sources, and Phase 4 tests cover it.
+
+### Wiki site matrix (`data/raw/sitematrix.json`)
+
+| Measure | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Numeric groups | 375 groups, 949 sites |
+| `specials` group | a list of 124 sites (all `lang` en, for example `abstractwiki`, `metawiki`) |
+| Sites in total | 1,073, equal to the file's `count` field |
+| Distinct `url` hosts | 1,073 |
+| Distinct `dbname` values | 1,073 |
+| Host to `dbname` mapping | one to one (no host with two dbnames, no dbname with two hosts) |
+| `auth.wikimedia.org` | not in the matrix. It appears only in `log` events on the replay (see below) |
+
+### Replay stream (`http://localhost:8099/v2/stream/recentchange`)
+
+Captured for 60 seconds with `curl -N` into the scratch directory: 9,528 events. The first 200 were
+used for the field profile and the rest for the late-event check.
+
+| Measure | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `type` values | `edit` 4,978, `categorize` 3,419, `log` 769, `new` 362 |
+| In scope (D8: `edit` or `new`) | 5,340 |
+| `server_name` values | 10 distinct. All edit and new events map to a site-matrix host, and `wiki` equals `dbname` for every one |
+| Out of matrix | `auth.wikimedia.org` (102 events, all `log`). Out of scope |
+| `meta.id` | present on 9,528 of 9,528 events, all distinct in the capture (D7) |
+| Top-level `id` | present on 9,240 events. Not used for dedupe |
+| `timestamp` | integer Unix seconds. `meta.dt` is ISO 8601 with milliseconds |
+| `bot` | boolean. Actor type is `bot` when true, else `human` (D8) |
+| SSE `id:` line | A JSON array of Kafka-style positions with a millisecond stamp, for example `[{"topic":"eqiad.mediawiki.recentchange","partition":0,"timestamp":1792021612166},{"topic":"codfw.mediawiki.recentchange","partition":0,"offset":-1}]`. It is opaque to the consumer and is stored and sent back as text |
+| Distinct SSE `id:` values | 6,606 over 9,528 events. Runs of identical ids occur (65 runs of 2, 21 of 3, and longer). The id is a position, not a per-event key |
+| Out-of-order arrivals | 6,231 events have a `timestamp` at or below the earlier maximum. Span of the capture: 1,565 seconds |
+| Late events (plan rule: watermark more than 10 minutes past window end) | 0 in the capture |
+
+Consequences for the design:
+
+- Dedupe on `meta.id` (D7) is the protection against replay of a shared SSE id after a reconnect. The
+  consumer cannot assume one id per event, so resume uses the committed id and relies on `meta.id` for exactly-once counting.
+- The capture has no late events, so Q7 reads 0 on the captured sample. The lateness rule is tested with synthetic events in Phase 7 (C4).
+- Whether the replay server honours `Last-Event-ID` was not tested here. The capture had no disconnect. Phase 7 tests the resume path against the fake server, and the
+  live replay check is an assumption until then.
+
+## Decision record status
+
+Evidence for each record is in the section above. The records are set to `status: accepted` in `.context/proposals/`.
+
+| ID | Record | Evidence in this file |
+|---|---|---|
+| D1 | `cd-1-deps` | Design only, no profile numbers |
+| D2 | `cd-1-lake-layout` | Design only |
+| D3 | `cd-1-money-type` | 0 values with more than 2 decimal places |
+| D4 | `cd-1-place` | 7,781 no-county rows, 57 no-state rows |
+| D5 | `cd-1-population` | Census counts, Connecticut codes, MF-10 rule |
+| D6 | `cd-1-stream-commit` | SSE id shape and repeats |
+| D7 | `cd-1-lateness-dedupe` | `meta.id` unique on the capture, late count 0 |
+| D8 | `cd-1-edit-scope` | `type` counts, `server_name` to site matrix mapping |
+| D9 | `cd-1-stream-config` | Replay URL reachable |
+| D10 | `cd-1-yaml-mart` | Design only |
+| D11 | `cd-1-dbt-io` | Design only |
+| n/a | `cd-1-transaction-key` | 0 duplicate transaction keys |
